window.docContents[174].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Associative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___1":{"contents":"Definition 2.2.1. An associative algebra over k is a vector space A over k together with a bilinear map\nA \\times A \\to A, (a, b) \\mapsto ab, such that (ab)c = a(bc).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.2. Algebras\tAssociative algebra","header":"Associative algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Associative-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___2___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Section-2___5-Quotients-___-heading-and-definition-of-quotient-algebra/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___5_heading___heading-1":{"contents":"Let A be an algebra and let I be a two-sided ideal in A. Then A/I is the set of (additive) cosets of I. Let \\pi : A \\to A/I be the quotient map. We can define multiplication in A/I by \\pi(a) \\cdot \\pi(b) := \\pi(ab).\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.5. Quotients\tSection 2.5 Quotients — heading and definition of quotient algebra","header":"2.5. Quotients","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Section-2___5-Quotients-___-heading-and-definition-of-quotient-algebra/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___5_heading___heading-1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebras-as-infinitesimal-Lie-groups-and-Lie___s-correspondence/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___14":{"contents":"Remark 2.9.14. Lie algebras were introduced by Sophus Lie (see Section 2.10) as an infinitesimal version of Lie groups (in early texts they were called \"infinitesimal groups\" and were called Lie algebras by Hermann Weyl in honor of Lie). A Lie group is a group G which is also a manifold (i.e., a topological space which locally looks like \\mathbb{R}^n) such that the multiplication operation is differentiable. In this case, one can define the algebra of smooth functions C^\\infty(G) which carries an action of G by right translations ((g \\circ f)(x) := f(xg)), and the Lie algebra \\operatorname{Lie}(G) of G consists of derivations of this algebra which are invariant under this action (with the Lie bracket being the usual commutator of derivations). Clearly, such a derivation is determined by its action at the unit element e \\in G, so \\operatorname{Lie}(G) can be identified as a vector space with the tangent space T_e G to G at e.\n\nSophus Lie showed that the attachment G \\mapsto \\operatorname{Lie}(G) is a bijection between isomorphism classes of simply connected Lie groups (i.e., connected Lie groups on which every loop contracts to a point) and finite dimensional Lie algebras over \\mathbb{R}. This allows one to study (differentiable) representations of Lie groups by studying representations of their Lie algebras, which is easier since Lie algebras are \"linear\" objects while Lie groups are \"nonlinear\". Namely, a finite dimensional representation of G can be differentiated at e to yield a representation of \\operatorname{Lie}(G), and conversely, a finite dimensional representation of \\operatorname{Lie}(G) can be exponentiated to give a representation of G. Moreover, this correspondence extends to certain classes of infinite dimensional representations.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.9. Lie algebras\tLie algebras as infinitesimal Lie groups and Lie's correspondence","header":"Lie algebras as infinitesimal Lie groups and Lie's correspondence","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebras-as-infinitesimal-Lie-groups-and-Lie___s-correspondence/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___14"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/End_LPAR_V_RPAR_-as-a-semisimple-representation/#Chapter3___Example3___1___2":{"contents":"Example 3.1.2. Let V be an irreducible representation of A of dimension n. Then Y = \\operatorname{End}(V), with action of A by left multiplication, is a semisimple representation of A, isomorphic to nV (the direct sum of n copies of V). Indeed, any basis v_1, \\ldots, v_n of V gives rise to an isomorphism of representations \\operatorname{End}(V) \\to nV, given by x \\to (xv_1, \\ldots, xv_n).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\tEnd(V) as a semisimple representation","header":"End(V) as a semisimple representation","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/End_LPAR_V_RPAR_-as-a-semisimple-representation/#Chapter3___Example3___1___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___22___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$\tSection 5.22: The characters of L_lambda","header":"5.22. The characters of  L_\\lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___22___heading-1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Theorem___-Connected-quiver-is-finite-type-iff-Dynkin/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___5_theorem":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.1. Problems\tTheorem: Connected quiver is finite type iff Dynkin","header":"Theorem: Connected quiver is finite type iff Dynkin","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Theorem___-Connected-quiver-is-finite-type-iff-Dynkin/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___5_theorem"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/#chapter-06___section-6-9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations","header":"6.9. Problems","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/#chapter-06___section-6-9"}});
